course: dictionary
lesson: 5799
title: A polite word for a woman
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Madame is a noun, a naming word. It is a polite title for a grown-up woman, like «Mrs» or «madam». Say it as ma-DAM. You will hear it most in French, but English speakers use it too.
explain: Read the words around it for clues. Josh says «Bonjour, madame» to the baker, a polite greeting. Compare «Hiya!», which is casual. Adding madame shows respect, so madame must be a polite title.
explain: In English, madame is used in fancy or foreign settings, such as «Madame Curie». Its short form is Mme, and its plural is Mesdames, for more than one woman.
fact: Madame means «madam» or «Mrs» in French.
fact: Madame is a noun, said as ma-DAM.
fact: Madame is a polite title for a grown-up woman, used to show respect.
fact: Older pupils in France say «madame» or «monsieur» to their teachers, while young children say la maîtresse or le maître.
fact: The short form is Mme, and the plural is Mesdames.
exercise: «Bonjour, madame» means hello to a woman. What does madame mean? | *Mrs or madam, a polite title for a woman | Hello, friend | A fancy cake | A ham sandwich
exercise: Which word is the short form of madame? | Mad | *Mme | Ma | Madam
exercise: What is the plural of madame, for more than one woman? | Madame-s | *Mesdames | Madamettes | Madames, with an extra s
test: In «Madame Curie», why is madame used? | *It is a polite title used in a fancy or foreign setting | It means cake | It means ten | It means a dog's name
test: How do you say madame? | *ma-DAM | mah-DEE | MAD-um | ma-DOM
exam: Which sentence uses madame politely? | Oi, you there! | *«Bonjour, madame, may I have a cake, please?» | Give me that cake now! | Hiya, mate!
